<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Balkh</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Bactra</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Balkh, known to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> as Bactra, is an ancient city in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>, near Mazar-i-Sharif, once the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bactria">Bactria</a>. It was a centre of Achaemenid, Greco-Bactrian, Kushan and early Islamic rule and a hub of Buddhist and Zoroastrian learning, later called the 'mother of cities'. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> sacked it in 1220, and it never recovered its former size.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/balkh.jpg" alt="Balkh" width="320" height="343" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: AhmadElhan · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Green_Mosque,_Balkh.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Balkh lies about 24 km north-west of Mazar-i-Sharif and about 74 km south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxus">Amu Darya</a>, at an elevation of about 365 m. French archaeologists found remains of a first settlement in the citadel, Bala Hissar, dating to the Early Iron Age, about 1500 to 1000 BCE. An earlier name, or the name of part of the city, was Zariaspa, which may come from the Zoroastrian fire temple Azar-i-Asp. The city's nickname is the mother of all cities.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The first historical reference is in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahabharata">Mahabharata</a>, which calls the place Valhika and says an important trade route passed through it. Balkh is the home town of Trapusa and Bahalika, the two merchants who, according to Buddhist scripture, became the Buddha's first disciples. Balkh was a refuge for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yazdegerd-iii">Yazdegerd III</a> when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> in the 7th century; they attacked it in 645, compelled it to pay tribute in 653, and the Umayyad caliph <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muawiya-i">Muawiya</a> reconquered it in 663.</p><p>In 1220 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a> sacked Balkh, massacred its inhabitants and levelled the buildings that could be defended. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marco-polo">Marco Polo</a>, probably referring to its past, described it as a noble city and a great seat of learning, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-battuta">Ibn Battuta</a>, visiting about 1333, found it dilapidated and uninhabited. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a> captured it in 1389 and destroyed its citadel, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shah-rukh">Shah Rukh</a> rebuilt in 1407. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-shaybani">Muhammad Shaybani</a>'s Uzbeks entered in 1506, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khanate-of-bukhara">Khanate of Bukhara</a> ruled it apart from Safavid rule from 1598 to 1601. In 1850 Dost Muhammad Khan took it for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">Emirate</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Balkh is in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tajiks">Tajiks</a> the majority of its people and Dari the main language. Much of the town consists of ruined buildings. Among its monuments are the Green Mosque, named for its green-tiled dome, and the Nine Domes Mosque, the earliest Islamic monument yet identified in Afghanistan. Buddhist structures have proved more durable than the Islamic buildings, among them Top-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rostam">Rustam</a>, a circular mound about 15 m high. Balkh did much for the development of Persian literature, and the poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rumi">Rumi</a> was born there in the 13th century.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Balkh” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the west Mahmud defeated the Qarakhanids near <b>Balkh</b> in 1008, annexed Khwarazm in 1017 and took Rayy from the Buyids in 1029.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ghaznavid-empire">Ghaznavid Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Ghurids came to control the cities of Herat, Ghazni and <b>Balkh</b>, the trade routes between Khurasan and India and, from the 1190s, the rich farmland of the Punjab and the Ganges plain, whose treasuries and temples yielded enormous booty.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ghurid-sultanate">Ghurid Sultanate · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Alexander the Great crossed the Hindu Kush in 329 BCE and conquered Bactria, placing garrisons at <b>Bactra</b>, Maracanda and Cyropolis and settling Greek mercenaries in new colonies along the Oxus and the Jaxartes; he also massacred the Greek community settled there before him.</blockquote><a href="/empire/greco-bactrian-kingdom">Greco-Bactrian Kingdom · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Yet under their dominion Buddhism was predominant, with Zoroastrian and Manichaean minorities and many Hindu adherents; <b>Balkh</b> had some 100 monasteries and 30,000 monks, the capital of Chaghaniyan five, and the Buddhas of Bamiyan were begun under Hephthalite rule.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hephthalite-empire">Hephthalite Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Bactria seceded from the Seleucid Empire under Diodotus I around 250 BCE; Euthydemus, a Magnesian Greek, seized its throne about 230 BCE and withstood a three-year siege of <b>Bactra</b> by Antiochus III, who in about 206 BCE recognised him and offered a daughter to his son Demetrius.</blockquote><a href="/empire/indo-greek-kingdom">Indo-Greek Kingdom · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Mahmud hurried back and, with war elephants and a disciplined professional army, defeated Nasr and Yusuf Qadir Khan near <b>Balkh</b> in January 1008.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kara-khanid-khanate">Kara-Khanid Khanate · Military</a></li></ul></section>
